// GRADUAL.disp — the gradual stream type checker, walked through
//
// One idea, built in nine steps: a type is a test that would take forever, so
// make the test a STREAM whose every step is cheap, and let every faster checker
// be a shortcut that must agree with the stream. Each step asks you to predict
// something before a test line reveals it. Cover the test lines and guess first;
// the guessing is where the understanding forms.
//
// Run, memory-capped (see CLAUDE.md):
//   timeout 300 systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=3G -p MemorySwapMax=512M npx tsx src/run.ts GRADUAL.disp
// Needs lib/machine.disp, lib/stream.disp and IDEAS.disp (the size-ordered enumeration of all trees).
open use raw "lib/prelude.disp" {}
open use raw "lib/list.disp" {}
open use raw "lib/machine.disp" {}
open use raw "lib/stream.disp" {}
open use raw "IDEAS.disp" {}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. a claim about infinitely many trees
// A type is a program that says yes or no to a tree. Here is Nat by shape: the
// leaf, or a fork whose left child is the leaf and whose right child is a Nat.
// (The library spells NatR with ==; shape tests cost fewer steps under the
// interpreter of step 3, and that is the only reason for the difference.)
rec NatR :: {v : Tree} -> Bool :=> if (is_zero v) { true } else if (is_fork v) { if (is_leaf v.fst) { NatR v.snd } else { false } } else { false }
test NatR 3 = true
test NatR (t t) = false

// Q: "succ : Nat -> Nat" is a claim about how many trees?
// A: every Nat. No single run checks it. But the members can be LISTED: Trees
// enumerates every tree smallest first, and a filter keeps the Nats.
test Trees.bounded(40).s_filter(NatR).s_take(3).s_items = [0, 1, 2]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2. the honest checker, and its one-sidedness
// IDEAS.disp's Arrow runs the claim on the listed members: filter the domain,
// map the function, ask the codomain about every output.
// Q: which of these two answers is a proof?
test Arrow NatR NatR 40 succ = true
test Arrow NatR NatR 40 ({n} => pair n n) = false
// A: only the false. It comes with a witness you can re-run: pair 1 1 is a fork
// whose left child is 1, not the leaf. The true only says "no counterexample
// among the trees the budget reached". Evidence, never a verdict.
//
// Q: what happens to Arrow if the DOMAIN recognizer loops on some input?
rec LoopR :: {v : Tree} -> Bool :=> if (is_zero v) { true } else { LoopR v }
// A: the filter calls LoopR natively on the second tree and never returns.
// There is deliberately no test line here: that run was measured, and it is
// killed at the memory cap. Everything from step 3 on exists to answer it.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3. the substrate's rules, with a fuel gauge
// To run a program for a bounded number of steps we need the evaluator as a
// program we control. Applying f to x has five cases:
//   leaf            x  =  stem x                      grows
//   stem a          x  =  fork a x                    grows
//   fork leaf b     x  =  b                           K: drops x unlooked at
//   fork (stem w) b x  =  (w x) (b x)                 S: copies x unlooked at
//   fork (fork w v) b x:  x leaf -> w,  x stem u -> v u,  x fork u u' -> b u u'
// Q: which rule is the only one that looks INSIDE x?
// A: the last one, triage. Keep it: it is why a placeholder can stand for every
// input in CHECK.disp, and why fuel is the only thing this interpreter adds.
answered :: {v : Tree} -> Res :=> pair "Answered" v
stuck :: Res := "Stuck"
is_answered :: Res -> Bool := {r} => r.fst == "Answered"
answer :: Res -> Tree := pair_snd
// run_s f x fuel: every rule firing spends one unit, and the fuel left comes
// back with the result so sub-runs share one budget (fuel bounds work, not depth).
rec run_s :: {f : Tree, x : Tree, fuel : Nat} -> Pair Res Nat :=> {
	let go := {a, b, left} => run_s a b left
	let bind := {rf, continue} => if (rf.fst.is_answered) { continue rf.fst.answer rf.snd } else { rf }
	let stop := {r, left} => pair r left
	let left0 := nat_pred fuel
	let rule_s := {w, b} => bind (go w x left0) ({wx, l1} => bind (go b x l1) ({bx, l2} => go wx bx l2))
	let rule_triage := {w, v, b} =>
		if (is_leaf x) { stop (answered w) left0 }
		else if (is_stem x) { go v (stem_child x) left0 }
		else { bind (go b x.fst left0) ({r, l1} => go r x.snd l1) }
	if (is_zero fuel) { stop stuck 0 }
	else if (is_leaf f) { stop (answered (t x)) left0 }
	else if (is_stem f) { stop (answered (t (stem_child f) x)) left0 }
	else {
		let a := f.fst
		if (is_leaf a) { stop (answered f.snd) left0 }
		else if (is_stem a) { rule_s (stem_child a) f.snd }
		else { rule_triage a.fst a.snd f.snd }
	}
}
run :: {f : Tree, x : Tree, fuel : Nat} -> Res :=> (run_s f x fuel).fst
test run succ 2 50 = answered 3
test run NatR 3 5000 = answered true
// Q: LoopR 0 answers under any fuel and LoopR 1 never. What does run say at 300?
test run LoopR 0 300 = answered true
test run LoopR 1 300 = stuck
// A: stuck, and it would be stuck at any fuel. Stuck is not "false": it is "not
// yet", and nothing in this file ever turns a stuck into a verdict.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4. a task: one application, as a stream that answers at most once
// A stream step must always return. A program run may not. Reconcile them by
// chopping the RUN: each step of a task runs the application from scratch with
// `next` of the previous fuel, yields the answer if it got one, otherwise skips.
// After yielding it is done. Restarting is what a run that cannot pause leaves
// you; with doubling fuel the restarts cost a constant factor.
restart_task :: {f : Tree, x : Tree, first_fuel : Nat, next : Nat -> Nat} -> Stream Tree :=> s_new ({st} => {
	let phase := st.fst
	if (phase == "Finished") { done }
	else {
		let r := run f x st.snd
		if (r.is_answered) { yield r.answer (pair "Finished" t) }
		else { skip (pair "Running" (next st.snd)) }
	}
}) (pair "Running" first_fuel)
rec add :: {a : Nat, b : Nat} -> Nat :=> if (is_zero a) { b } else { succ (add (nat_pred a) b) }
dbl :: Nat -> Nat := {n} => add n n
// Q: how many items does the task for `succ 2` ever yield? For `LoopR 1`?
test (restart_task succ 2 8 dbl).bounded(10).s_take(2).s_items = [3]
test (restart_task LoopR 1 8 dbl).bounded(6).s_take(1).s_items = []
// A: one and zero. And the second line RETURNED: six steps, each a bounded run,
// then the budget ended the stream. The program is partial; the stream is
// total. That is the whole trick. Everything after this is bookkeeping.
//
// Q: run_s stops when the fuel is gone. Why can a task not CONTINUE from there?
// A: the work in progress sat on the host's call stack, gone once run_s returned.
// lib/machine.disp keeps it in a value: the pending application plus a stack of
// frames saying what to do with its result, the loop of step 3 turned inside out.
// m_advance runs such a machine for k steps and hands the state back.
test m_result (m_advance (m_start succ 2) 100) = 3
test m_finished (m_advance (m_start LoopR 1) 300) = false
// lib/stream.disp's task advances one machine by the same quantum every step and
// yields its answer once. Nothing restarts, so there is no fuel schedule.
// Q: LoopR 1, quantum 8, six steps: how much of ITS run has each form done?
test (task succ 2 8).bounded(10).s_take(2).s_items = [3]
test (task LoopR 1 8).bounded(6).s_take(1).s_items = []
// A: the restart form ran 8 + 16 + ... + 256 steps and got 256 deep once; this one
// ran 48 steps of a single run and is still 48 deep, ready for the 49th.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5. many tasks at once: the fair merge
// A stream of streams, flattened fairly. Each round draws one inner stream from
// the source, then lets a POLICY step some of the live ones. Answers wait in a
// buffer and leave one per step. A policy returns (answers, survivors).
// Round-robin steps the head and sends it to the back:
advance_head :: {live : List (Stream V)} -> Pair (List V) (List (Stream V)) :=> {
	let inner := live.head
	let r := inner.s_step
	if (r.is_done) { pair [] live.tail }
	else if (not r.yielded) { pair [] (live.tail.append([inner.s_with r.skip_state])) }
	else { pair [r.y_val] (live.tail.append([inner.s_with r.y_state])) }
}
// Map steps every live stream: round-robin applied to each one alone. The calls
// are independent of each other, which is what a parallel evaluator would use.
rec advance_all :: {live : List (Stream V)} -> Pair (List V) (List (Stream V)) :=>
	if (live.is_nil) { pair [] [] }
	else {
		let one := advance_head [live.head]
		let rest := advance_all live.tail
		pair (one.fst.append(rest.fst)) (one.snd.append(rest.snd))
	}
s_merge_with :: {source : Stream (Stream V), policy : List (Stream V) -> Pair (List V) (List (Stream V))} -> Stream V :=> s_new ({st} => {
	let src := st.fst
	let live := st.snd.fst
	let buf := st.snd.snd
	if (not buf.is_nil) { yield buf.head (pair src (pair live buf.tail)) }
	else {
		let s := src.s_step
		let pulled := if (s.is_done) { pair src live }
			else if (s.yielded) { pair (src.s_with s.y_state) (live.append([s.y_val])) }
			else { pair (src.s_with s.skip_state) live }
		if (pulled.snd.is_nil) { if (s.is_done) { done } else { skip (pair pulled.fst (pair [] [])) } }
		else {
			let adv := policy pulled.snd
			skip (pair pulled.fst (pair adv.snd adv.fst))
		}
	}
}) (pair source (pair [] []))
// Q: the merge is `done` in exactly one situation. Which?
// A: the source is exhausted AND no live stream remains. Keep that: it is the
// only way a Proved will ever come out of a stream.
s_from_list :: {items : List V} -> Stream V :=> s_new ({xs} => if (xs.is_nil) { done } else { yield xs.head xs.tail }) items
// Three finite streams, both policies: same items, order aside.
three := s_from_list [s_from_list [1, 2], s_from_list [3], s_from_list [4, 5]]
merged_rr := (s_merge_with three advance_head).bounded(40).s_take(9).s_items
merged_all := (s_merge_with three advance_all).bounded(40).s_take(9).s_items
test merged_rr.length = 5
test merged_all.length = 5
test [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].all({n} => merged_rr.member(n)) = true
test [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].all({n} => merged_all.member(n)) = true

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6. the general Pi: a stream of witnesses
// The claim "fn : Pi A B", with A the domain recognizer and B the relation
// between an input and its output. One task per candidate tree: the whole check
// is ONE closed program, so partial A, B and fn compose by ordinary application
// inside it, and only the boundary around the candidate is a stream. The
// witness stream keeps the candidates whose check came back true.
witnesses_over :: {source : Stream Tree, A : Tree, B : Tree, fn : Tree, policy : Tree, quantum : Nat} -> Stream Tree :=> {
	let check := {a} => if (A a) { not (B a (fn a)) } else { false }
	let tasks := source.s_map({a} => (task check a quantum).s_map({b} => pair a b))
	(s_merge_with tasks policy).s_filter({p} => p.snd == true).s_map({p} => p.fst)
}
witnesses :: {A : Tree, B : Tree, fn : Tree, policy : Tree, quantum : Nat} -> Stream Tree :=> witnesses_over Trees A B fn policy quantum
nat_cod := {_a, y} => NatR y
// Round-robin gives each task rare turns; map gives every task a turn every
// round. With the restart form the fuel schedule had to match the policy
// (doubling for rare turns, a constant added for frequent ones); a paused run
// takes the same quantum under either. That decoupling is what the machine buys.
// Q: the first witness against pair n n : Nat -> Nat? (0 maps to pair 0 0 = 1, a Nat.)
test (witnesses NatR nat_cod ({n} => pair n n) advance_head 64).bounded(300).s_take(1).s_items = [1]
test (witnesses NatR nat_cod ({n} => pair n n) advance_all 64).bounded(40).s_take(1).s_items = [1]
// Now the case Arrow could not survive. LoopR accepts 0 and loops on everything
// else; this function returns a stem, never a Nat.
// Q: does the witness stream find 0, given that every other probe spins forever?
test (witnesses LoopR nat_cod ({_n} => t t) advance_head 64).bounded(120).s_take(1).s_items = [0]
test (witnesses LoopR nat_cod ({_n} => t t) advance_all 64).bounded(12).s_take(1).s_items = [0]
// A: yes, in both forms. The spinning probes sit in the live list burning their
// fuel; fairness means 0's probe still gets its turns. This is the measured
// difference between the two checkers: Arrow dies here, this one answers.
// And a claim that is true:
test (witnesses NatR nat_cod succ advance_head 64).bounded(150).s_take(1).s_items = []
// Q: is that last line a proof that succ : Nat -> Nat?
// A: no. The budget ran out with the source still going. Silence within a
// budget is evidence. Even unbounded silence would only say "no input where
// succ halts outside Nat", weaker than membership, because a function that
// never halts on some input is silent there too.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7. when the stream ends: exhaustion, and the three verdicts
// Over a FINITE source the merge can reach done. Bool has two members.
bool_cod := {_a, y} => Bool y
bools := s_from_list [true, false]
// Q: what does the witness stream of `not : Bool -> Bool` do, and how would
// you tell that apart from a budget running out?
exhausted := (witnesses_over bools Bool bool_cod not advance_head 64).bounded(50).s_take(1)
test exhausted.s_items = []
test exhausted.s_rest.s_step.is_done = true
test is_zero exhausted.s_rest.snd.fst = false
// A: it ends with budget to spare: the rest is done AND the budget, the first
// component of bounded's state, is not zero. That peek into bounded's state is
// the honest label the library does not yet carry as a type. "Ended by
// exhaustion" and "ended by budget" are different facts, and only the first is
// a proof. Making that a grade on the stream is the next library step.
// The three verdicts, read off a bounded witness stream:
verdict :: {ws : Bounded (Stream Tree)} -> Tree :=> {
	let taken := ws.s_take(1)
	if (not taken.s_items.is_nil) { pair "Refuted" taken.s_items.head }
	else if (is_zero taken.s_rest.snd.fst) { "Open" }
	else { "Proved" }
}
test verdict ((witnesses_over bools Bool bool_cod not advance_head 64).bounded(50)) = "Proved"
test verdict ((witnesses_over bools Bool bool_cod ({b} => pair b b) advance_head 64).bounded(50)) = pair "Refuted" true
test verdict ((witnesses NatR nat_cod succ advance_head 64).bounded(50)) = "Open"
// Q: why is Proved reachable only through a finite source here?
// A: because done needs the source exhausted, and over Trees that never
// happens. Every checker in CHECK.disp and lib/tests/confirmer.test.disp is a
// way to say Proved WITHOUT reaching done: one run on a placeholder, a case
// split, an induction licence. Each is sound exactly when it agrees with this
// stream on every candidate the stream would ever draw. That is the contract.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8. grades: the same stream, other verdict algebras
// Bool is one way to total up a stream of checks. Any algebra with a `times`,
// its unit, and an absorbing `zero` (so the fold can stop early) will do. This
// one combinator is s_all, the failure count, the worst cost, or the
// three-valued verdict, depending only on what you hand it.
rec s_fold_absorb :: {stream : Bounded (Stream V), times : P -> P -> P, one : P, zero : P, measure : V -> P} -> P :=> {
	let step := stream.s_step
	if (step.is_done) { one }
	else if (not step.yielded) { s_fold_absorb (stream.s_with step.skip_state) times one zero measure }
	else {
		let here := measure step.y_val
		if (here == zero) { zero } else { times here (s_fold_absorb (stream.s_with step.y_state) times one zero measure) }
	}
}
// Q: with times = and, one = true, zero = false, which library function is this?
test s_fold_absorb ((s_from_list [1, 2, 3]).bounded(9)) and true false NatR = true
test s_fold_absorb ((s_from_list [1, t t, 3]).bounded(9)) and true false NatR = false
// A: s_all, early exit included. Now count failures instead of stopping at the
// first: the witness stream over the first four Nats, folded with plus (there
// is no absorbing element for plus, so pass one that never occurs).
first_nats := s_from_list [0, 1, 2, 3]
fails := (witnesses_over first_nats NatR nat_cod ({n} => pair n n) advance_head 64).bounded(200)
test s_fold_absorb fails add 0 "None" ({_w} => 1) = 3
// pair n n fails on 3 of the first 4 Nats: a distance from membership. An
// optimizer can descend a number where it cannot descend a Bool.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9. where this leaves the design
// What you have seen, one line each:
//   a type is a recognizer; its members are a stream                    (1)
//   the honest checker refutes with witnesses, never proves infinity     (2)
//   an evaluator with a fuel gauge makes a partial program bounded       (3)
//   a paused run advanced a quantum per step is a task: total over partial (4)
//   a fair merge with a pluggable policy runs every task at once         (5)
//   the general Pi is the witness stream of one closed check per input   (6)
//   done means exhaustion, the only stream-born Proved                   (7)
//   the verdict algebra is a parameter: Bool, counts, costs, three values (8)
// Q: where would a faster checker plug in?
// A: as a claim about this stream, "the witness stream is empty forever", backed
// by an argument instead of a run. CHECK.disp makes that argument with one run
// on a placeholder; the confirmer adds case splits and an induction licence.
// They are shortcuts, and their contract is agreement with steps 6 and 7 on
// every candidate. Nothing above them is ever more than a shortcut.
// Still missing, most valuable first: a native stepper for the machine
// (m_advance is the readable spec, and slow); a grade on streams for "ended by
// exhaustion" versus "ended by budget"; a queue with cheap rotation in place of
// append.
